    {
      "id": "19",
      "type": "related_content",
      "attributes": {
        "id": 19,
        "relationship_type": "antonym",
        "body": "だ follows nouns and な-adjectives, with a meaning similar to the English 'is.' For instance, これはペンだ translates to 'This is a pen.' On the other hand, じゃない (a contraction of ではない) is used to create the negative of such expressions. For example, これはペンじゃない translates to 'This isn't a pen.'",
        "first_relatable": {
          "id": 1,
          "slug": "だ",
          "title": "だ",
          "meaning": "To be, Is",
          "level": "JLPT5",
          "type_pascal": "GrammarPoint",
          "type_snake": "grammar_point"
        },
        "second_relatable": {
          "id": 49,
          "slug": "じゃない",
          "title": "じゃない",
          "meaning": "Is not, Isn't",
          "level": "JLPT5",
          "type_pascal": "GrammarPoint",
          "type_snake": "grammar_point"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "51",
      "type": "related_content",
      "attributes": {
        "id": 51,
        "relationship_type": "related",
        "body": "がある indicates the existence or possession of something. For example, ペンがある means 'There is a pen (on the table, etc.)' or 'I have a pen.' On the other hand, だ expresses assertion or determination similar to 'is/are' in English. In contrast to the がある example, ペンだ would mean 'It (=the thing we are looking at) is a pen.'",
        "first_relatable": {
          "id": 13,
          "slug": "がある",
          "title": "がある",
          "meaning": "To be, There is",
          "level": "JLPT5",
          "type_pascal": "GrammarPoint",
          "type_snake": "grammar_point"
        },
        "second_relatable": {
          "id": 1,
          "slug": "だ",
          "title": "だ",
          "meaning": "To be, Is",
          "level": "JLPT5",
          "type_pascal": "GrammarPoint",
          "type_snake": "grammar_point"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "1333",
      "type": "related_content",
      "attributes": {
        "id": 1333,
        "relationship_type": "related",
        "body": "よ is a common sentence-ending particle used to add emphasis, often indicating the speaker is sharing new information (e.g., 面白いよ = 'It's interesting, I'm telling you!'). だ is a grammatical form expressing assertion, similar to 'is' or 'am.' These two are often combined, especially in casual or masculine speech (元気だよ = 'I'm feeling good!'). In softer or more feminine speech, the だ is frequently omitted (e.g., 元気よ).",
        "first_relatable": {
          "id": 78,
          "slug": "よ",
          "title": "よ",
          "meaning": "Emphasis (Sentence ending particle)",
          "level": "JLPT5",
          "type_pascal": "GrammarPoint",
          "type_snake": "grammar_point"
        },
        "second_relatable": {
          "id": 1,
          "slug": "だ",
          "title": "だ",
          "meaning": "To be, Is",
          "level": "JLPT5",
          "type_pascal": "GrammarPoint",
          "type_snake": "grammar_point"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "18",
      "type": "related_content",
      "attributes": {
        "id": 18,
        "relationship_type": "synonym",
        "body": "だ and です both function like the English 'is.' The main difference is politeness: だ is casual and used in plain speech, while です is polite. だ can't be used after い-adjectives, but です can, acting as a general politeness marker. You may still see だ in set expressions like ～だと思います, even in polite sentences.",
        "first_relatable": {
          "id": 1,
          "slug": "だ",
          "title": "だ",
          "meaning": "To be, Is",
          "level": "JLPT5",
          "type_pascal": "GrammarPoint",
          "type_snake": "grammar_point"
        },
        "second_relatable": {
          "id": 2,
          "slug": "です",
          "title": "です",
          "meaning": "To be, Is",
          "level": "JLPT5",
          "type_pascal": "GrammarPoint",
          "type_snake": "grammar_point"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "121",
      "type": "related_content",
      "attributes": {
        "id": 121,
        "relationship_type": "related",
        "body": "だった・でした and だ are all used to assert or identify something. The difference among them is in formality and tense. だ is casual and present tense (similar to the English 'is'/'am'), while だった・でした is past tense ('was'), with だった being casual (the past tense of だ) and でした being polite (the past tense of です).",
        "first_relatable": {
          "id": 40,
          "slug": "だった-でした",
          "title": "だった・でした",
          "meaning": "Was, Were (Past tense) ",
          "level": "JLPT5",
          "type_pascal": "GrammarPoint",
          "type_snake": "grammar_point"
        },
        "second_relatable": {
          "id": 1,
          "slug": "だ",
          "title": "だ",
          "meaning": "To be, Is",
          "level": "JLPT5",
          "type_pascal": "GrammarPoint",
          "type_snake": "grammar_point"
        }
      }
